course: dictionary
lesson: 7252
title: Pier: one word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pier is a long platform built out from the shore, out into the sea or a lake, held up on posts. People walk along it, and boats can stop at it. Read the words around the word to find which meaning fits.
explain: «In strong wind on the beach, stay away from the edge of piers and harbour walls, because big waves and gusts can knock you into the sea.» The clues are strong wind, big waves and knock you into the sea. So here a pier is the walkway out over the water, and standing at its edge in bad weather is not safe.
explain: A pier can also be a strong pillar that holds up a bridge. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A pier is a long platform built out from the shore into the sea or a lake.
fact: Piers are held up on posts.
fact: «Tombstoning» means jumping from piers, cliffs or bridges into water.
fact: Jumping from a pier is dangerous, because tides change the depth and hidden rocks or rubbish can lie under the surface.
fact: A «rip» forms where water rushes back out through a narrow gap, and it can form beside a pier.
exercise: A pier is a long platform built out from the shore, on posts. What is a pier? | *A long platform out from the shore, on posts | A deep hole in the sand | A type of seaweed that grows on rocks
exercise: «Tombstoning means jumping from piers, cliffs or bridges into water.» Why is this dangerous? | Fish will bite you when you land | The water is always too warm to swim in | Piers are too wide to walk on | *Hidden rocks or rubbish may lie under the water
exercise: «The old pier held up one end of the bridge.» What does pier mean here? | A boat that carries cars | A big wave in the sea | A long walk along the beach | *A strong pillar that holds up a bridge
test: Why should you stay away from the edge of a pier in strong wind? | The pier gets taller in the wind | You might get a sunburn | *Big waves and gusts can knock you into the sea | Wind makes the pier too pretty to see
test: What is a rip? | A kind of fish and chip | A tall rock on the beach | *A narrow gap where water rushes back out to sea | A fun ride at the seaside
exam: Which statement about a pier is true? | It can only be found in a swimming pool | *It is a long platform built out from the shore | It is always a fish and chip shop | It is a deep hole in the sand
